    <div id="hermes-definitions" class="authority-block">
      <h3>What are the four Hermes directions?</h3>
      <dl class="authority-definitions">
        <dt>U.S. Logistics</dt><dd>Carrier, dealer, shipper, auction and vehicle-transport coordination for U.S. logistics workflows.</dd>
        <dt>Marketing</dt><dd>Organic social media, content and paid-distribution systems designed to help businesses build visibility and qualified demand.</dd>
        <dt>Business Academy</dt><dd>Practical operations-focused education built around real workflows, role clarity and measurable execution.</dd>
        <dt>AI &amp; Web Systems</dt><dd>Websites, CRM, automation and AI-enabled tools that organize information, handoffs and repetitive business processes.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div id="hermes-comparison" class="authority-block">
      <h3>How do the Hermes paths compare?</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Comparison of Hermes business directions">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Direction</th><th>Best fit when you need</th><th>Typical next step</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>U.S. Logistics</td><td>Carrier support, vehicle movement, dealer/shipper coordination</td><td>Choose your logistics role and open the matching intake</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Marketing</td><td>More qualified online visibility and a repeatable content pipeline</td><td>Review the marketing direction and request a strategy fit</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Business Academy</td><td>Structured operational learning tied to practical work</td><td>Review the program and application requirements</td></tr>
            <tr><td>AI &amp; Web Systems</td><td>Website, CRM, automation or AI workflow improvement</td><td>Describe the system or website outcome you need</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>There is no universal “best” direction. A logistics operator may need dispatch or transport coordination first; on the other hand, a service business with weak lead flow may benefit more from marketing or a conversion-focused website. Another perspective is operational: if the bottleneck is repetitive manual work rather than demand, automation can be the higher-priority investment.</p>
    </div>

    <div id="hermes-questions" class="authority-block authority-qa">
      <h3>Why does Hermes keep these directions in one ecosystem?</h3>
      <p>Business bottlenecks are connected. Demand generation without an operating process creates leakage, while automation without a clear workflow automates confusion. Keeping the directions connected lets a business start with the immediate bottleneck and add adjacent systems only when they are useful.</p>

      <h3>How should you choose a Hermes service?</h3>
      <p>Start with the outcome you need in the next 30–90 days. If the problem is moving freight or vehicles, begin with Logistics. If the problem is visibility or lead flow, begin with Marketing. If the problem is capability-building, review the Academy. If the problem is manual work, fragmented data or an outdated website, begin with AI &amp; Web Systems.</p>

      <h3>What evidence should you expect before making a decision?</h3>
      <p>Look for a clear scope, current public terms, specific workflow explanations, measurable acceptance criteria and evidence that can be verified. Hermes separates verified production capabilities from previews and concepts so a visitor can distinguish what is live from what is still being developed.</p>
    </div>

    <aside id="hermes-sources" class="authority-block" aria-labelledby="sources-title">
      <h3 id="sources-title">Standards and authoritative references</h3>
      <p>This guide uses public standards and regulatory references where they apply. For search and structured-data implementation, see <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs" rel="noopener">Google Search Central</a> and <a href="https://schema.org/" rel="noopener">Schema.org</a>. For U.S. motor-carrier authority and safety records, use the <a href="https://www.fmcsa.dot.gov/" rel="noopener">Federal Motor Carrier Safety Administration</a>. These sources define standards or public records; they do not endorse Hermes.</p>
    </aside>

    <div class="authority-block">
      <h3>Conclusion: choose the bottleneck, not the category</h3>
      <p>The practical recommendation is to choose the Hermes direction that addresses the constraint closest to revenue, delivery or operating capacity. Then measure the result before adding another layer. That keeps the engagement understandable and avoids buying technology, marketing or training that the business is not ready to use.</p>
